### What is event time?

Event time is the timestamp carried inside the event itself, for example the point-of-sale time embedded in a transaction. You tell Flink how to extract it with a timestamp assigner inside a watermark strategy. Results are deterministic and replayable because the grouping depends on the data, not on when you run the job.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.WatermarkStrategy;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.TumblingEventTimeWindows;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;
import java.time.Duration;

public class EventTimeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(
                WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
                    Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                .withTimestampAssigner(
                    (txn, ts) -> txn.getPosTimeMillis()))
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
            .sum("amount")
            .print("Per-minute total");

        env.execute("NBI Event-Time Settlement Report");
    }
}
```

```text
Per-minute total> Transaction{accountId=ACC-001, amount=125000.00}
Per-minute total> Transaction{accountId=ACC-002, amount=45000.00}
// Replaying the same data a week later produces identical results
```

### What is processing time?

Processing time uses the operator machine's system clock. A processing-time window of one minute groups whatever records arrive in the next sixty seconds of wall-clock time. It needs no watermarks and no timestamps, so it has the lowest latency and simplest setup.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.TumblingProcessingTimeWindows;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;

public class ProcessingTimeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // No watermarks, no timestamp extraction
        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .window(TumblingProcessingTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
            .sum("amount")
            .print("Ops dashboard");

        // WARNING: non-deterministic results
        // Replaying the same data at different speed gives different totals
        env.execute("NBI Ops Dashboard");
    }
}
```

### What is ingestion time?

Ingestion time assigns the timestamp at the source operator when the record enters Flink, then behaves like event time downstream with automatic watermarks. It is more stable than processing time because all downstream operators agree on one timestamp, but it ignores the true delay between when the event happened and when it reached Flink.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.WatermarkStrategy;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import java.time.Duration;

public class IngestionTimeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Simulate ingestion time by assigning current time at source
        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(
                WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
                    Duration.ZERO)
                .withTimestampAssigner(
                    (txn, ts) -> System.currentTimeMillis())) // ingestion time
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
            .sum("amount")
            .print("Ingestion-time total");

        env.execute("NBI Ingestion-Time Demo");
    }
}
```

### How are the three time semantics different?

| Feature | Event Time | Processing Time | Ingestion Time |
|---|---|---|---|
| Source | Timestamp in the record | Operator machine clock | Flink source operator clock |
| Deterministic | Yes (replayable) | No (depends on speed) | Partially (stable within run) |
| Watermarks needed | Yes | No | Automatic |
| Latency | Higher (waits for watermark) | Lowest | Medium |
| Out-of-order handling | Yes (configurable) | No | No |
| Use case | Financial reports, audits | Rough dashboards | Rarely used in modern Flink |

```java
// Same transaction, three different time semantics:
// Transaction: ACC-001, Rs5000, POS time = 10:00:00
// Arrived at Flink source at 10:00:02
// Processed by operator at 10:00:05

// Event time window (10:00 - 10:01): includes this transaction
// Processing time window (10:00 - 10:01): includes at 10:00:05
// Ingestion time window (10:00 - 10:01): includes at 10:00:02

// If replayed at 11:00:
// Event time: still in 10:00 window (deterministic)
// Processing time: now in 11:00 window (non-deterministic!)
// Ingestion time: depends on replay speed
```

### How do watermarks work with event time?

Watermarks are special markers that flow with the stream saying: I believe I have seen all events up to time T. When a watermark crosses a window's end time, the window fires. The `forBoundedOutOfOrderness` strategy generates watermarks assuming events can be at most a certain duration late.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.WatermarkStrategy;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.WatermarkGenerator;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.WatermarkOutput;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.Watermark;

public class CustomWatermarkDemo {
    // Custom watermark generator for NBI transactions
    public static class NbiWatermarkGenerator
            implements WatermarkGenerator<Transaction> {

        private long maxTimestamp = Long.MIN_VALUE;
        private static final long MAX_DELAY = 5000L; // 5 seconds

        @Override
        public void onEvent(Transaction txn, long eventTimestamp,
                WatermarkOutput output) {
            maxTimestamp = Math.max(maxTimestamp, eventTimestamp);
        }

        @Override
        public void onPeriodicEmit(WatermarkOutput output) {
            // Watermark = max seen timestamp minus allowed delay
            output.emitWatermark(
                new Watermark(maxTimestamp - MAX_DELAY));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Watermark flow:
  Event(10:00:03) ──▶ Watermark(09:59:58)
  Event(10:00:07) ──▶ Watermark(10:00:02)
  Event(10:00:10) ──▶ Watermark(10:00:05)
  // Window [10:00:00 - 10:01:00) fires when watermark >= 10:01:00
```

### What are common mistakes with time semantics?

The most common mistake is using processing time for financial aggregations that must be reconciled or audited. The same data replayed at a different speed produces different window results, which breaks reconciliation against the card network.

```java
// WRONG: processing time for settlement report
stream.keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
    .window(TumblingProcessingTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .sum("amount"); // non-deterministic: replay changes results!

// CORRECT: event time for settlement report
stream.assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(
        WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
            Duration.ofSeconds(5))
        .withTimestampAssigner((txn, ts) -> txn.getPosTimeMillis()))
    .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .sum("amount"); // deterministic: same data = same result always
```

### When should you use each time semantic?

Use event time when the result must be correct, auditable, or replayable, because the business cares about when the thing actually happened. Use processing time when you only need a rough, low-latency operational metric and nobody will audit the exact grouping. Ingestion time is rarely the explicit choice today.

```text
Decision guide:
  Financial settlement report?  --> Event time (must reconcile)
  Regulatory audit feed?        --> Event time (must be replayable)
  Fraud detection windows?      --> Event time (event order matters)
  Ops health dashboard?         --> Processing time (rough is fine)
  "How many TPS right now?"     --> Processing time (simplest)
  General streaming job?        --> Event time (safe default)
```

### What should a senior engineer know about Flink time semantics?

A senior engineer should know that event time is almost always the right default for financial workloads, that watermark generation must account for source-specific delays (like mobile network latency), that idle sources can stall watermarks and need `withIdleness()`, and that the allowed-lateness and side-output mechanisms handle events that arrive after the watermark has passed.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.WatermarkStrategy;
import java.time.Duration;

public class SeniorTimeConfigDemo {
    public static WatermarkStrategy<Transaction> nbiWatermarkStrategy() {
        return WatermarkStrategy
            .<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
            .withTimestampAssigner((txn, ts) -> txn.getPosTimeMillis())
            .withIdleness(Duration.ofMinutes(1)); // handle idle partitions
    }
}
```
